,"topics":["hacktoberfest","python","test","testing","unit-testing"],"visibility":"public","forks":2969,"open_issues":955,"watchers":13369,"default_branch":"main","permissions":{"admin":false,"maintain":false,"push":false,"triage":false,"pull":true}},"body":"Versions :\r\npytest version: 4.5.0\r\nOS version : 4.13.0-43-generic #48~16.04.1-Ubuntu\r\n\r\nHello, there! I am on my way to learn how to use pytest, and here is my question!\r\n\r\nSample code:\r\n```python\r\nimport pytest\r\n\r\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"test_input,expected\", [\r\n    (\"3+5\", 8),(\"6*8\", 43),pytest.param(\"4*5\", 20, marks=pytest.mark.xfail),\r\n    pytest.param(\"6*9\", 42, marks=pytest.mark.xfail),\r\n])\r\n\r\ndef test_eval(test_input, expected):\r\n    assert eval(test_input) == expected\r\n```\r\n\r\nI used the above code to understand what are the marks in pytest, and get used to with 'parametrize'. I regard 'parametrize' is special testing way in pytest. So, I want to be used to it.\r\nBack to the code, [1] how can I use 'id parameter' in pytest.params? I think in the above code, except explicit marks parameters, all are values. [1]How can I use 'id parameter' with the above sample code?\r\n\r\nAnother question is about 'xfail'. So, this only can result in 'xfail(expected fail(actual fail, but categorized as 'xfail'' or 'Fail(when it success, unexpected success)'. That's how I can understand 'xfail' now.  \r\n\r\n[2]In the result from pytest, simply, 'pass', 'fail', 'xfail', 'error', are these four signs from pytest? Can we have 'xpass' or customized test result sign in pytest?\r\n\r\nIn the pytest, I checked 'skip', 'skipif', 'xfail', and 'parametrize' as built-in marks. However, since I am more curious about the test result, it would be great to know the answer of [2] question.","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pytest-dev/pytest/issues/5426/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":